  data:  # 1c8e
    kind: work_item  # 1c8e
    title: "defusedcsv (and other drop-in stdlib wrappers) are absent from the taint catalog - true flows previously reached only by substring coincidence are now missed"  # 1c8e
    status: todo_hard  # 1c8e
    priority: 2  # 1c8e
    parent:  # 1c8e
    tags:  # 1c8e
    - analysis_quality  # 1c8e
    - adr_0017  # 1c8e
    isbefore: []  # 1c8e
    duplicate_of: []  # 1c8e
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 1c8e
    pr_ref:  # 1c8e
    description: "FOUND BY THE WI-zazul VALIDATION COHORT 2026-08-02, as the one genuine cost of\nthat fix. Filed untagged as a regression sub-item per the bakeoff-validation\nlifecycle.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. WI-zazul replaced substring module matching with component-aware\nmatching. On the 9-repo fresh-substrate A/B that removed 6 flows, and 5 of the 6\nare exactly the false positives the item filed. The sixth is not:\n\n    [pretix] sink='writer'\n             sink_symbol=python:defusedcsv:0-0:csv.writer:external_symbol\n\nVERIFIED: the Python catalog carries module 'csv' name 'writer' zone host_fs,\nand 'defusedcsv' is NOT catalogued at all. The old predicate matched it as\n'csv' in 'defusedcsv' -- a three-character substring coincidence. The new\npredicate correctly reports that defusedcsv is a different module.\n\nWHY THIS IS A REAL LOSS AND NOT A CORRECT REMOVAL. defusedcsv is a drop-in\nreplacement for the stdlib csv module that escapes leading =, +, -, @ to prevent\nCSV-injection when the output is opened in a spreadsheet. defusedcsv.writer IS a\ncsv write. The flow was a true positive; the old code reached it by accident.\n\nWHY THE FIX IS NOT TO LOOSEN THE PREDICATE. Substring matching was not\nrecognising defusedcsv; it was matching three characters, and would equally have\nmatched csv_exporter, mycsvlib or notcsv. Module identity belongs in the\ncatalog, not in a fuzzy comparison. Reverting would restore this one true flow\nand the five false ones with it.\n\nPROPOSED FIX. Add defusedcsv to the Python sink catalog as a csv-equivalent\n(module 'defusedcsv', names matching the csv surface it re-exports: writer,\nDictWriter, and whatever else it wraps -- read the package, do not assume the\nsurface). Then AUDIT for the same shape: third-party drop-in wrappers around a\ncatalogued stdlib module whose flows were previously reached only by substring\ncoincidence. Candidates to check rather than presume: defusedxml (vs xml),\nujson/orjson/simplejson (vs json), regex (vs re), and any `secure*`/`safe*`/\n`defused*` prefixed package wrapping a catalogued module.\n\nHOW TO FIND THE REST CHEAPLY: for every catalogued module M, the old predicate\nmatched any module containing M as a substring. Diffing the pre-WI-zazul and\npost-WI-zazul cohort outputs enumerates exactly which of those were being\nexercised -- on this cohort it was one. A wider pool would surface more, and\nthat diff is the instrument rather than guesswork.\n\nSCOPE, STATED HONESTLY: 1 flow of 178 on this cohort (0.6%). This is the\nunder-matching tradeoff WI-zazul documented in the predicate's docstring,\nobserved rather than predicted."  # 1c8e
    fields: {}  # 1c8e
- op: discuss  # 0282
  at: '2026-08-05T04:16:43Z'  # 0282
  by: agent  # 0282
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0282
  clock: 2  # 0282
  nonce: '0282'  # 0282
  message: "ENUMERATED, which is what the item asked for instead of guesswork. Ran the filing own instrument - for every catalogued module M, find emitted modules that CONTAIN M as a substring, i.e. exactly what the retired predicate matched and the component-aware one does not - across the 9-repo cohort. 304 distinct candidate rows: go 273, python 29, javascript 2. Positive control held: the filed defusedcsv case is in the pool (python:defusedcsv:0-0:csv.writer, x4 in pretix). NOTE ON THE INSTRUMENT: my first display used most_common(25) and truncated the known case behind go rows with counts in the hundreds - a silent cap that read as a null. Re-run unbounded. THREE GROUPS FALL OUT. (1) GENUINE DROP-INS, catalogue them: defusedcsv .csv.writer x4; orjson .loads/.dumps/.orjson.loads/.orjson.dumps x13; ujson .dumps x1. All are json/csv drop-ins whose whole purpose is API compatibility with a catalogued stdlib module. (2) GENUINE UNCATALOGUED PRIMITIVES that are NOT wrappers and belong in their own item: django.core.files.storage .default_storage.open/save/delete/url/exists x21 - a real filesystem abstraction that matched only by the substring file - plus tarfile.open and zipfile. (3) COINCIDENCES, and they are the evidence WI-zazul was right: sys.path.insert matched by sys, jsonschema.validate by json, nest_asyncio.apply by asyncio, django.conf.locale.LANG_INFO.update by locale, django.contrib.staticfiles.finders.find by file, and best of all pretix.presale.views.customer.ProfileView.as_view matched by file inside the word Profile. The go pool is almost entirely this shape: net/http/httptest (INV-karud own filed counterexample), sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime matched by runtime, github.com/go-openapi/strfmt matched by fmt. THE PLAN SPECULATIVE CANDIDATES DO NOT APPEAR: defusedxml, regex and simplejson are absent from the cohort entirely, so adding them would be exactly the presuming the surface this item warns against. REMAINING WORK is a bounded catalogue addition for group 1 - and the item own instruction stands: read each package surface rather than assuming it, because a drop-in that re-exports only part of the stdlib API would gain false sinks for the rest."  # 0282
